<PublicationPage title="Editorial policy" description="How ServerHi documents operational advice, checks changing claims and records corrections.">
  <p>Updated November 25, 2026</p>
  <h2>Walkthroughs and source-based reporting</h2>
  <p>In a walkthrough, look for the operating system, software version, inputs and the output that confirms success. A lab observation applies to the stated environment. In release and advisory reporting, the upstream announcement establishes what the publisher said. A distribution package, rollout date or performance result on a particular host needs its own evidence.</p>
  <p>For example, the <a href="/posts/backup-strategies/">PostgreSQL backup article</a> describes how a restored record was checked. The <a href="/posts/github-actions-self-hosted-runners-setup/">runner cost example</a> shows a calculation with dated prices and an explicit usage assumption. Check the provider’s rates when making a purchase.</p>
  <h2>Corrections and updates</h2>
  <p>Use the <a href="/contact/">correction channels</a> to identify a specific paragraph or command. For a reproducible issue include the smallest example you can share safely. A substantial correction should explain what changed and how the earlier advice affected the reader. The original publication date stays visible alongside the update date.</p>
  <h2>Attribution</h2>
  <p>Articles identify their author or the <a href="/author/">shared editorial byline</a>. Product documentation and named announcements are linked where readers need to verify a claim. Operational decisions belong to the person responsible for the system and its data.</p>
</PublicationPage>
